@page "/history"

<PageTitle>History · SIQS</PageTitle>

<header class="page-head">
    <SectionIcon Name="history" />
    <div>
        <h1>History</h1>
        <p>The ideas and people behind the quadratic sieve.</p>
    </div>
</header>

<section class="history-timeline">
    <article class="history-entry">
        <Portrait Src="/img/history/fermat.jpg" Alt="Pierre de Fermat engraving"
                  Credit="Engraving by François de Poilly — public domain, via Wikimedia Commons" Initials="PF" Hue="210" />
        <div>
            <h2>1600s — Fermat's difference of squares</h2>
            <p>Pierre de Fermat factored numbers by writing N = X<sup>2</sup> − Y<sup>2</sup> = (X−Y)(X+Y). It works
                well only when factors are close together, but the identity x<sup>2</sup> ≡ y<sup>2</sup> (mod N) behind
                it powers every modern sieve.</p>
            </div>
        </article>
        <article class="history-entry">
            <Portrait Src="/img/history/kraitchik-icm-1932.jpg" Alt="Maurice Kraitchik with colleagues at the 1932 ICM"
                      Credit="Maurice Kraitchik — back row, just right of centre, facing left — with colleagues at the International Congress of Mathematicians, Zürich 1932. Photo: Johannes Meiner, ICM Zürich 1932 (ETH-Bibliothek) — public domain, via Wikimedia Commons"
                      Initials="MK" Hue="275" Scene="true" />
            <div>
                <h2>1920s — Kraitchik's congruences</h2>
                <p>Maurice Kraitchik relaxed Fermat's idea: instead of an exact difference of squares, combine many small
                    congruences x<sup>2</sup> ≡ residue (mod N) and multiply subsets into squares.</p>
                </div>
            </article>
            <article class="history-entry">
                <Portrait Alt="Michael Morrison and John Brillhart" Initials="MB" Hue="35" />
                <div>
                    <h2>1970 — CFRAC breaks F₇</h2>
                    <p>Michael Morrison and John Brillhart used the continued-fraction method to factor the seventh Fermat
                        number F₇ = 2¹²⁸ + 1 (39 digits), the landmark machine-factorization of its era and the template of
                        “relations + linear algebra”.</p>
                    </div>
                </article>
                <article class="history-entry">
                    <Portrait Alt="John Dixon" Initials="JD" Hue="110" />
                    <div>
                        <h2>1975/1981 — Dixon's random squares</h2>
                        <p>John Dixon's method — pick random x, keep the smooth x<sup>2</sup> mod N — gave the first rigorous
                            runtime analysis for this family of algorithms.</p>
                        </div>
                    </article>
                    <article class="history-entry">
                        <Portrait Src="/img/history/pomerance.jpg" Alt="Carl Pomerance at a whiteboard"
                                  Credit="Photo: Eli Burakian / Dartmouth College, 2019" Initials="CP" Hue="190" Scene="true" />
                        <div>
                            <h2>1981–82 — Pomerance invents the quadratic sieve</h2>
                            <p>Carl Pomerance replaced random values with the polynomial x<sup>2</sup> − N sieved over an interval,
                                letting one addition of log p replace trial division. QS became the fastest general-purpose method of
                                the 1980s–early 1990s.</p>
                            </div>
                        </article>
                        <article class="history-entry">
                            <Portrait Src="/img/history/montgomery.jpg" Alt="Peter Montgomery"
                                      Credit="Photo: Dcoetzee — CC0, via Wikimedia Commons" Initials="PM" Hue="320" />
                            <div>
                                <h2>1980s — MPQS and the record chase</h2>
                                <p>Variants with many polynomials (multiple-polynomial QS, with key contributions by Peter Montgomery;
                                    large-prime variations; later the self-initializing variant SIQS used by this app) plus distributed
                                    volunteers pushed records past 100 digits.</p>
                                </div>
                            </article>
                            <article class="history-entry">
                                <Portrait Src="/img/history/lenstra.jpg" Alt="Arjen Lenstra"
                                          Credit="Photo: Rama — CC BY-SA 2.0 FR, via Wikimedia Commons" Initials="AL" Hue="15" />
                                <div>
                                    <h2>1994 — RSA-129 and the squeamish ossifrage</h2>
                                    <p>The 129-digit RSA challenge number from Martin Gardner's 1977 Scientific American column fell to a QS
                                        effort led by Derek Atkins, Michael Graff, Arjen Lenstra and Paul Leyland, with about 600 volunteers
                                        contributing idle machine time over eight months — decoding “THE MAGIC WORDS ARE SQUEAMISH OSSIFRAGE”.
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                            </article>
                            <article class="history-entry">
                                <Medallion Initials="GN" Hue="230" />
                                <div>
                                    <h2>Mid-1990s — the number field sieve takes over</h2>
                                    <p>For numbers beyond roughly 100–110 digits the general number field sieve (GNFS) is asymptotically faster;
                                        RSA-129 was QS's high-water mark. QS remains the algorithm of choice in the double-digit-to-~100-digit
                                        range — exactly this app's territory.</p>
                                        <p><a href="/learn">Learn the method</a> · <a href="/factorize">Factor a number</a></p>
                                    </div>
                                </article>
                            </section>

